<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The floating dictation card — RADICALLY MINIMAL.

  ## What is present at rest, and what is not

  At rest this card contains the transcript and one mic control. That is all.
  Removed by the 2026-10 design pass:

    * the "Dicta" title TextView — the card only ever appears because the user
      just tapped a Dicta trigger, so it named the app to someone who had just
      named it themselves;
    * the always-visible four-icon action row (Copy / Screenshot / Send /
      Close), which is now @id/action_row and starts GONE;
    * the full-bleed mic Button, which is now a centred pill sharing its row
      with the overflow trigger.

  Kept, because the layout is not the only consumer: @id/status_text,
  @id/dictated_text, @id/mic_button, @id/attachment_note, @id/copy_button,
  @id/screenshot_button, @id/share_button, @id/close_button and
  @id/dictate_card all still exist with the same ids and the same view types.
  DictateActivity and the unit suite address them by id, and so does in-flight
  work on another branch.

  ## 🔴 How a first-time user finds Send

  Three independent routes, deliberately — a hidden action with one route is a
  feature that exists only in the author's head:

    1. @id/more_button is ALWAYS VISIBLE, at rest, next to the mic. It is a
       ringed overflow control with contentDescription AND tooltipText ("More
       actions" / "Hide actions"), and tapping it reveals @id/action_row. It is
       the only other thing on the card besides the transcript and the mic, so
       it cannot be missed.
    2. The row AUTO-REVEALS the moment the transcript stops being empty —
       DictateActivity watches @id/dictated_text. So after the very first
       utterance the actions are simply on screen, and the user never has to
       discover anything. They are hidden only in the state where every one of
       them would refuse ("Nothing to copy yet…").
    3. @id/status_text, which is the first line of the card, names the route in
       words while the row is hidden ("Tap ⋯ for Copy, Send, Screenshot").

  ## Why still a ScrollView

  Unchanged reason: the window is a floating dialog with WRAP_CONTENT height,
  and on a landscape phone (~411dp usable, minus the 12dp window inset top and
  bottom) the column can be taller than the window. Without a scroll container
  the bottom of the card is clipped off rather than scrolled. Pinned by
  FloatingWindowSizeTest.theCardScrollsRatherThanClipping.
-->
<ScrollView xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
    android:background="@drawable/card_bg"
    android:clipToOutline="true">

    <LinearLayout
        android:id="@+id/dictate_card"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:orientation="vertical"
        android:padding="20dp">

        <!--
          ========================= THE STATUS STRIP =========================
          🔴 ISSUE #9: @id/status_text USED TO LIVE IN THIS ROW and no longer
          does. It was maxLines=1 + ellipsize=end, sharing a 801px strip with
          the 72dp meter, which left it 555px — about 30 characters. The two
          longest strings it carries are 154 and 145 characters
          (@string/stt_fell_back_retrying, @string/stt_tier_remote_only), so
          the user never saw the half of the sentence that says what happens
          NEXT ("…the server transcript is on its way"). No amount of maxLines
          rescues it inside this row: four lines of 555px is still ~123
          characters. It therefore sits BELOW the strip at full card width,
          where four lines hold ~194 characters. Pinned by
          OverlayMinimalLayoutTest.theLongestStatusStringIsNotTruncated.

          This row now carries two things, both GONE at rest. Both were
          RESERVED stubs
          for in-flight work on another branch; that branch (remote STT) has
          landed and both are now BOUND to real state. The reservation held —
          the merge was the mechanical swap the stubs were shaped for — so what
          follows documents the live contract, not a promise:

            @id/level_meter   a 4dp horizontal mic-level meter, 72dp x 4dp,
                              placed FIRST in the strip so it reads
                              left-to-right as "input is arriving" before the
                              words do. Now a ProgressBar (style
                              ?android:attr/progressBarStyleHorizontal) with
                              @drawable/meter_progress — the reserved
                              @drawable/meter_track_bg track under a
                              @color/dicta_accent level. Bound by
                              DictateActivity.onLevel. Works with no partials:
                              deliberately independent of @id/dictated_text.

            @id/engine_badge  an 11sp pill (@drawable/badge_bg,
                              @color/dicta_on_accent_container at 9.84:1)
                              naming which engine produced the current text.
                              Bound by DictateActivity.showEngine(fromRemote)
                              to @string/stt_remote_done or
                              @string/stt_local_label. Still placed LAST, now
                              simply so the meter leads; the older reason ("so
                              it does not shift the status text") retired with
                              the #9 move — there is no longer any status text
                              in this row to shift. It carries a
                              contentDescription of its own because a short
                              badge is not self-describing to a screen reader.
                              (Reserved as @id/engine_badge, built on the
                              branch as @id/engine_label; this id won the
                              merge. See the view's own comment.)

          🔴 Do not encode engine or in-flight state in colour alone: the badge
          exists so there is always a WORD.

          OverlayMinimalLayoutTest pins this strip: the membership and ORDER of
          the three children, that the meter and the badge are GONE in the
          layout's at-rest default, that both are labelled, and — since the
          reservation was filled — that both actually become visible and
          carry text/level once a dictation drives them.
          =================================================================
        -->
        <LinearLayout
            android:id="@+id/overlay_status_strip"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:orientation="horizontal"
            android:gravity="center_vertical"
            android:baselineAligned="false">

            <!--
              The microphone level meter — the design's reserved 72dp x 4dp slot,
              now carrying the remote-STT branch's real implementation. The swap
              is the one this file's header prescribed: a ProgressBar styled
              ?android:attr/progressBarStyleHorizontal, whose progressDrawable
              (@drawable/meter_progress) layers the reserved
              @drawable/meter_track_bg track under a @color/dicta_accent level.

              🔴 Not decoration. In the remote-only degradation tiers no partial
              text ever appears while recording, so this bar is the ONLY
              on-screen evidence that the microphone is live. Without it
              "nothing is moving" is indistinguishable from a dead capture — and
              a dead capture is a real state here, because Android enforces its
              one-capture-at-a-time policy by silently feeding the loser exact
              zeros. DictateActivity.onLevel binds progress; onClientSilenced
              flips it to INVISIBLE (not GONE) so the strip does not reflow when
              another app steals the mic.

              GONE is the LAYOUT default only — it is the at-rest state, before
              a dictation starts, not an "unbound stub" any more.
            -->
            <ProgressBar
                android:id="@+id/level_meter"
                style="?android:attr/progressBarStyleHorizontal"
                android:layout_width="72dp"
                android:layout_height="4dp"
                android:layout_marginEnd="10dp"
                android:max="100"
                android:progress="0"
                android:progressDrawable="@drawable/meter_progress"
                android:contentDescription="@string/level_meter_desc"
                android:visibility="gone" />

            <!-- Pushes the badge to the trailing edge now that the status
                 text no longer takes the row's spare weight. -->
            <Space
                android:id="@+id/status_strip_spacer"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="1dp"
                android:layout_weight="1" />

            <!--
              Which engine produced the text currently in the box. The server
              and the phone disagree, sometimes materially, so the user has to
              be able to tell them apart rather than trust whatever appeared.
              DictateActivity.showEngine(fromRemote) binds the text.

              🔴 This view was @id/engine_label on the remote-STT branch and
              @id/engine_badge in the design pass's reservation. ONE id
              survived the merge — this one — because the design side owns more
              references (this layout, @string/engine_badge_desc,
              @drawable/badge_bg, OverlayMinimalLayoutTest's strip ledger) and
              because "badge" names what it renders as. The branch's two
              references were renamed to match.

              maxLines=1: the strip is one row, and the two bound strings are
              short. If longer copy ever lands here it must shrink, not reflow
              the card — the long-form fallback explanation belongs in
              @id/status_text, which already takes the weight and ellipsizes.
            -->
            <TextView
                android:id="@+id/engine_badge"
                android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginStart="8dp"
                android:paddingStart="8dp"
                android:paddingEnd="8dp"
                android:paddingTop="2dp"
                android:paddingBottom="2dp"
                android:background="@drawable/badge_bg"
                android:textSize="11sp"
                android:maxLines="1"
                android:ellipsize="end"
                android:textColor="@color/dicta_on_accent_container"
                android:contentDescription="@string/engine_badge_desc"
                android:visibility="gone" />
        </LinearLayout>

        <!--
          The live status line — the card's first words.

          🔴 FULL CARD WIDTH, AND NOT ONE LINE. This is the #9 fix. It used to
          be a weighted child of @id/overlay_status_strip above, capped at
          maxLines=1 with ellipsize=end, which on this 1080px device left it
          555px — roughly 30 characters — to render strings up to 154.
          Everything after the first clause was replaced by an ellipsis,
          including the half that tells the user what happens next.

          maxLines=4 at full width is ~194 characters, which clears the longest
          string with margin. ellipsize=end is kept only as a backstop for copy
          added later; if it ever actually engages, this view needs another
          line, not a shorter sentence — the card is a ScrollView and can
          afford the height. OverlayMinimalLayoutTest measures the real longest
          string against the real inflated width rather than trusting this
          arithmetic.
        -->
        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/status_text"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_marginTop="2dp"
            android:text="@string/dictate_hint"
            android:textSize="12sp"
            android:maxLines="4"
            android:ellipsize="end"
            android:lineSpacingExtra="1dp"
            android:textColor="@color/dicta_on_surface_variant" />

        <!--
          The transcript: the hero, and the reason the card exists. 17sp rather
          than the platform default because this is the content, not a form
          field, and minLines 3 rather than 4 — with the title and the action
          row gone the card no longer needs a tall box to look balanced.
          @color/dicta_on_surface measures 14.01:1 on the card.
        -->
        <EditText
            android:id="@+id/dictated_text"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_marginTop="12dp"
            android:minLines="3"
            android:gravity="top"
            android:hint="@string/dictate_text_hint"
            android:textSize="17sp"
            android:lineSpacingExtra="3dp"
            android:textColor="@color/dicta_on_surface"
            android:textColorHint="@color/dicta_on_surface_variant"
            android:background="@drawable/input_bg"
            android:padding="14dp"
            android:inputType="textMultiLine|textCapSentences" />

        <!-- Shown only once a screenshot has actually been attached. The note is
             not decoration: many share targets drop EXTRA_TEXT when a stream is
             present, so the user needs to know Copy is the reliable text path. -->
        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/attachment_note"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_marginTop="8dp"
            android:textSize="12sp"
            android:textColor="@color/dicta_on_surface_variant"
            android:visibility="gone"
            android:text="@string/screenshot_attached_note" />

        <!--
          The resting control row: the mic, and the way to everything else.

          @id/mic_button stays a Button (not an ImageButton) on purpose. Four
          places in DictateActivity assign `micButton.text`, another branch is
          editing that file right now, and the label is what keeps the recording
          state off colour-only footing ("Speak" / "Stop"). It is styled as an
          accent pill with the mic glyph as a compound drawable instead.
        -->
        <LinearLayout
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:orientation="horizontal"
            android:layout_marginTop="16dp"
            android:gravity="center_vertical">

            <Button
                android:id="@+id/mic_button"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="56dp"
                android:layout_weight="1"
                android:background="@drawable/mic_pill_bg"
                android:drawableStart="@drawable/ic_mic"
                android:drawableTint="@color/dicta_on_accent"
                android:drawablePadding="8dp"
                android:paddingStart="22dp"
                android:paddingEnd="22dp"
                android:stateListAnimator="@null"
                android:textColor="@color/dicta_on_accent"
                android:textSize="15sp"
                android:textAllCaps="false"
                android:text="@string/start_dictation" />

            <!-- The overflow. 48dp square = the minimum touch target, and the
                 ONLY always-visible route to the hidden actions, so it carries
                 both a contentDescription and a tooltipText and DictateActivity
                 swaps both when it toggles. -->
            <ImageButton
                android:id="@+id/more_button"
                android:layout_width="48dp"
                android:layout_height="48dp"
                android:layout_marginStart="12dp"
                android:src="@drawable/ic_more"
                android:scaleType="center"
                android:background="@drawable/ghost_button_bg"
                android:tint="@color/dicta_on_surface_variant"
                android:contentDescription="@string/more_actions"
                android:tooltipText="@string/more_actions" />
        </LinearLayout>

        <!--
          The on-demand actions. GONE at rest; revealed by @id/more_button or
          automatically once the transcript is non-empty (see the header).

          Still icon-only and still 4 children, unchanged ids and unchanged
          order. Each is 48dp tall (the minimum touch target; the previous 52dp
          was fine too, 48 is what the extra row of chrome can afford) and each
          keeps BOTH contentDescription and tooltipText — a control that is
          sometimes invisible needs its label more than one that is always on
          screen, not less.

          🔴 DictateActionViewTypesTest asserts this row's child-id set as a
          ledger: adding a fifth action here without updating that list turns
          the suite red. @id/more_button is deliberately NOT a child of this row
          (it must stay visible when the row is hidden), and is covered instead
          by OverlayMinimalLayoutTest's icon-control ledger.
        -->
        <LinearLayout
            android:id="@+id/action_row"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:orientation="horizontal"
            android:layout_marginTop="10dp"
            android:visibility="gone">

            <ImageButton
                android:id="@+id/copy_button"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="48dp"
                android:layout_weight="1"
                android:src="@drawable/ic_copy"
                android:scaleType="center"
                android:background="@drawable/icon_button_bg"
                android:tint="@color/dicta_on_surface"
                android:contentDescription="@string/copy"
                android:tooltipText="@string/copy" />

            <ImageButton
                android:id="@+id/screenshot_button"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="48dp"
                android:layout_weight="1"
                android:layout_marginStart="8dp"
                android:src="@drawable/ic_screenshot"
                android:scaleType="center"
                android:background="@drawable/icon_button_bg"
                android:tint="@color/dicta_on_surface"
                android:contentDescription="@string/screenshot"
                android:tooltipText="@string/screenshot" />

            <ImageButton
                android:id="@+id/share_button"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="48dp"
                android:layout_weight="1"
                android:layout_marginStart="8dp"
                android:src="@drawable/ic_send"
                android:scaleType="center"
                android:background="@drawable/icon_button_bg"
                android:tint="@color/dicta_on_surface"
                android:contentDescription="@string/share"
                android:tooltipText="@string/share" />

            <ImageButton
                android:id="@+id/close_button"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="48dp"
                android:layout_weight="1"
                android:layout_marginStart="8dp"
                android:src="@drawable/ic_close"
                android:scaleType="center"
                android:background="@drawable/icon_button_bg"
                android:tint="@color/dicta_on_surface"
                android:contentDescription="@string/close"
                android:tooltipText="@string/close" />
        </LinearLayout>
    </LinearLayout>
</ScrollView>
